  "hints": [
    "Each cell needs both its old state and enough information to store its new state during the first pass.",
    "Use temporary codes: 2 for live→dead and 3 for dead→live. Both 1 and 2 count as originally live.",
    "Count eight neighbors from values in (1,2), assign transition codes, then take every cell modulo 2 in a second pass.",
    "The encoded board supplies the old generation to every cell while still retaining the next generation in place."
  ],
  "editorial": "Two bits of state fit in each integer cell. During the first pass, values 1 and 2 both mean originally live, while 2 and 3 record a changed next state. This prevents row-order cascades. A final modulo pass maps unchanged/transition codes to the next 0/1 generation. The independent oracle reads only an untouched input matrix and writes a separate result.",
